  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 145 AM CDT Mon Aug 3 2026\n\nThis workweek we will see a fairly decent break from the heat, \nbut not before another hot day today -- especially far \nsouthwest Kansas. Latest short term model guidance is highly \nsuggestive of afternoon temperatures in the 104 to 106F range \nfrom Liberal to Elkhart north to around Syracuse. It will be a \ndry heat, though, with dewpoints generally in the upper 40s to \nlower 50s. This will result in Heat Index values several degrees\nlower than the actual air temperature. Despite this, we have \ncollaborated with NWS Amarillo in the issuance of a Heat \nAdvisory mainly due to the actual air temperature and heat \nstress potential to agriculture interests. A fairly strong south\nwind 15 to 25 mph should alleviate some of this heat stress, \nbut direct insolation will occur all day with large scale \nsubsidence resulting in essentially no clouds. \n\nA rather strong shortwave trough will be moving east across \nthe far northern CONUS starting Tuesday, and this will bring a \npolar front south into Kansas. This front will then stall out \nand become the focus for multiple shower and thunderstorm \nchances through about Thursday Night, at which point the frontal\nzone will dissolve as the summer subtropical high builds back \nin. The latest NBM shows 40 to 60% chance of 1/4\" or more QPF \nover a 48-hr period from Tuesday Night to Thursday Night. The \nfrontal zone in place and outflow boundaries will result in a \ndifficult temperature forecast, and this is seen in the NBM with\n10-90th percentile ensemble spread of 10 to 15 degrees for \nhighs Tuesday and Wednesday with 10th percentile high \ntemperatures as low as the mid 70s across portions of west \ncentral Kansas. \n\nThe pattern will revert back to favoring very hot temperatures \nas we head into next weekend with 100 to 105F highs \nincreasingly likely...particularly August 9th and beyond.",
